2026-08-24 @ 13:05 - [Fix - package-helm-bundle.sh now pre-fills the bundled .config.example's IMAGE_TAG]:
  User reported: whoever deploys a Helm bundle has to manually know and
  type in the correct IMAGE_TAG, since .config.example ships with a
  generic "1.0.0" placeholder and the real .config (with the actual
  pushed b<N> tag) is deliberately never bundled -- risking a stale or
  wrong image being deployed if that value isn't communicated out of
  band correctly.
  Fixed in build-scripts/package-helm-bundle.sh: added
  patch_config_example_image_tag(), which reads only IMAGE_TAG (nothing
  else -- no credentials) from this checkout's real .config, if one
  exists, and patches just that one line of the staged .config.example
  via a `^IMAGE_TAG=`-anchored sed substitution. Reuses the same
  defensive "don't fail if .config doesn't exist yet" pattern
  default_output_path() already had for the output filename. The
  generated README.txt now says explicitly whether IMAGE_TAG was
  pre-filled (and with what value) or must be filled in manually.
  Verified live both ways: with .config present (IMAGE_TAG=1.0.0b18),
  the bundled .config.example and README.txt both reflect the real tag,
  and grep confirmed IMAGE_PREFIX and the surrounding comment block
  (which also mentions "IMAGE_TAG" in prose) were untouched; with
  .config temporarily moved aside, the bundle falls back to the shipped
  placeholder with no error, unchanged from before this fix.

