2026-07-09 @ 20:10 - [Feat - traffic-generator deployed via Helm, on a separate node from the app]:
  The traffic-generator previously only existed as a Docker Compose service
  (docker-compose.yml's `traffic:`); it had no Kubernetes/Helm equivalent.
  Added helm/php-demo/templates/traffic-deployment.yaml: a plain Deployment
  (not StatefulSet -- no persistent identity/storage needs), gated by the new
  trafficGenerator.enabled value (default false in values.yaml; deploy.sh
  always sets it true, mirroring how compose.sh always includes the
  standalone traffic service). Reuses the existing ServiceAccount for
  registry-pull access; TARGET_URL points at the in-cluster Service DNS name
  (http://<fullname>:<service.port>). Container env vars mirror the
  TRAFFIC_*/plain-name split generator.py actually reads (MIN_ACTION_DELAY_SECS,
  LOG_LEVEL unprefixed; TRAFFIC_ANONYMOUS_RATIO, TRAFFIC_CONCURRENT_SESSIONS,
  TRAFFIC_SLOWDOWN_* prefixed) -- same split already used by
  docker-compose.yml's traffic: service.
    - Not in the same pod as the app: separate Deployment/container/pod
      identity entirely, never in the StatefulSet's pod.
    - Different node than the app: podAntiAffinity
      (trafficGenerator.antiAffinity) targets the app pod's new
      app.kubernetes.io/component: app label with
      topologyKey: kubernetes.io/hostname. antiAffinity.required (default
      true) is a hard requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution
      constraint; set to false for a soft preferred* rule on single-node
      clusters (kind/minikube/demo VM), where a hard constraint would leave
      the pod permanently Pending.
    - Label-selector landmine: Kubernetes matchLabels is subset/superset
      matching, not exact-equality, so a selector on just the common
      name+instance labels also matches ANY other workload sharing those
      two labels (both workloads get them via the shared php-demo.labels
      helper). Applied the fix -- an explicit app.kubernetes.io/component
      label ("app" on the StatefulSet's pod template, "traffic-generator"
      on the new Deployment's pods) -- to the Deployment's own selector and
      its anti-affinity labelSelector, but initially missed that
      templates/service.yaml and templates/service-headless.yaml carry the
      exact same selector, unfixed.
  Files changed (first pass): helm/php-demo/templates/traffic-deployment.yaml
  (new), helm/php-demo/templates/statefulset.yaml (added the component: app
  pod label), helm/php-demo/templates/_helpers.tpl (added
  php-demo.trafficFullname), helm/php-demo/values.yaml (added
  image.trafficGenerator and the trafficGenerator block),
  build-scripts/deploy.sh (generate_values() now emits
  image.trafficGenerator + trafficGenerator.env.* from the existing
  TRAFFIC_* .config variables, and always sets trafficGenerator.enabled:
  true), CLAUDE.md, README.md.
  Verified via `helm lint` and `helm template` (no live cluster in this
  sandbox): the Deployment rendered with the correct anti-affinity block
  (both required and preferred modes), the StatefulSet's selector/pod
  identity was unaffected by the additive label, and a full render with
  deploy.sh's generated values.local.yaml (simulated via a test harness
  sourcing generate_values() with fake .config values) succeeded end to end.

  **Bug fixed same day, found on the user's real deploy:** first live
  deploy on a 2-node k3s cluster returned intermittent 502 Bad Gateway
  from Traefik. `kubectl get endpoints php-demo-php-demo` showed TWO
  endpoints -- the real app pod and the traffic-generator pod -- because
  the main ClusterIP Service's selector (templates/service.yaml) matches
