2026-07-07 @ 15:00 - [Feat - traffic-generator/: synthetic user traffic for the demo shop]:
  New traffic-generator/ directory: a standalone container that continuously
  generates realistic shop traffic against the app, cycling through the
  full demo user roster (shuffled once per pass) so trouble/empty_basket/
  locked all fire regularly instead of only by chance. Per user: login, a
  random number of random actions (browse with occasional brand/capability/
  page filters, view a product, add to basket, view the basket, or
  complete a checkout -- weighted so basket/checkout activity is common
  without crowding out plain browsing), then logout; the admin account also
  occasionally visits the admin diagnostic pages. A blocked login (locked,
  403) or an empty-basket checkout attempt are tolerated, expected outcomes,
  not errors.
  generator.py uses only the Python standard library (urllib,
  http.cookiejar) -- no requirements.txt, no pip install, no dependency
  layer. This was forced, not stylistic: PyPI (files.pythonhosted.org) was
  unreachable from the build sandbox used to write this (a 403 even after
  fixing an initial TLS-interception cert error, i.e. a network policy
  block, not a cert problem) -- requests would have been the more usual
  choice otherwise.
  Two real urllib bugs found and fixed while writing this: (1)
  HTTPRedirectHandler.redirect_request returning None (to deliberately not
  follow a 3xx so the caller can inspect Location itself -- e.g. to tell a
  successful login apart from a re-rendered form) does not suppress the
  exception as the stdlib docs imply -- urlopen still raises HTTPError for
  the 3xx via the default error handler. (2) The app's own 403/404
  responses raise the same way. Both are now caught in _do_request() and
  converted into a normal Response object (HTTPError is itself a valid
  response-like object: .code, .headers, .read()).
  Dockerfile: python:3.12-slim, non-root `traffic` user, no apt-get/pip
  layers at all given the stdlib-only design -- image is ~177MB, almost
  entirely the base image.
  TRAFFIC_ENABLED=false keeps the container up but idle, same pattern as
  APMIA_DEPLOY in dx-o2-agents/entrypoint.sh. Pacing
  (TRAFFIC_MIN/MAX_ACTION_DELAY_SECS, TRAFFIC_MIN/MAX_SESSION_DELAY_SECS)
  and TRAFFIC_LOG_LEVEL are new .config variables; TARGET_URL is hardcoded
  to the Compose service name in docker-compose.yml, matching the existing
  APMIA_PHP_COLLECTOR_HOST/APMIA_BTL_HOST convention.
  docker-compose.yml: new `traffic` service, depends_on apachephp
  (service_started -- no healthcheck exists to depend on service_healthy;
  the generator's own wait_for_app() polls /health with a bounded timeout
  instead).
  build-scripts/{build,push,compose}.sh: traffic-generator built/pushed
  alongside the other two images; compose.sh's images_present() and
  load_config() updated to include it and its new TRAFFIC_* vars.
  Verified end to end against the real stack, not just a syntax check:
  built and ran via compose.sh build/up -d; confirmed real traffic in
  Apache's own access log with correct status codes per action (403 for
  the blocked locked login, 302 for successful logins/redirects/checkout,
  200 for pages); confirmed the trouble use case's 5000-sequential-DB-read
  slowdown is visible in the generator's own action timing (~2.3s gap vs.
  the configured 0.2-0.5s delay in a fast-paced test run); zero unexpected
  errors or tracebacks across a multi-minute run cycling all 14 users
  several times.
  CLAUDE.md, README.md, .config.example: documented the new component, its
  configuration, and the urllib gotchas.

